course: dictionary
lesson: 15483
title: Striking: One Word, Many Meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the words around them tell you which one is right. Read the sentence first. «The ball, striking the window, made a loud crack.» A ball cannot be ringing the hours, so striking means hitting something hard.
explain: «The clock is striking six.» Clocks do not hit anything. The clue is the clock and six, so striking means ringing the hours.
explain: «She wore a striking red dress.» The dress did not hit anything. The clue is that it is easy to notice, so striking means eye-catching. Try your meaning in the sentence: does it still make sense? It does.
fact: Striking is an adjective, and it is also the -ing form of the verb strike (strike, strikes, struck, striking).
fact: «Striking» can mean hitting something hard, as in «the ball striking the window».
fact: «Striking» can mean very easy to notice, as in «a striking red dress».
fact: A clock striking six rings six times, so there are only five gaps between the six strikes.
fact: Lightning often strikes tall things, so do not shelter under a lone tree in a storm.
exercise: «The ball, striking the window, made a loud crack.» What does striking mean here? | Stopping work to ask for better pay | Eye-catching and bright | *Hitting something hard | Ringing the hours
exercise: «The clock is striking six.» What is the clock doing? | *Ringing the hours | Running slowly | Hitting the wall | Stopping for the night
exercise: A clock strikes six times at six o'clock. How many gaps are there between the six strikes? | 6 | 7 | *5 | 4
test: «Her striking blue coat could be seen from across the park.» What does striking mean here? | Stopping work | *Eye-catching, very easy to notice | Ringing the hours | Hitting something hard
test: Why should you not shelter under a lone tree in a storm? | Trees give no shade | Trees make the rain colder | *Lightning often strikes tall things | Trees are too heavy to move
exam: Which word is NOT a form of the verb strike? | *stroll | striking | struck | strikes
